    ('pivot', "// pivot — the most recent confirmed swing high / low, carried forward.\n//\n// THIS MODULE EXISTS TO PACKAGE A WORKAROUND, and that is the whole point of it\n// being in a library rather than re-derived per port.\n//\n// Pine writes `ta.valuewhen(hih, high[pivotLookup], 0)` — sampling the source\n// series at a RUNTIME offset. That is not expressible here: a history offset must\n// be a literal (`OS2006`). The equivalence that rescues it: `ta.pivothigh(high,\n// l, r)` returns the pivot's VALUE at the confirming bar, and at that bar the\n// pivot's value IS `high[r]`. So sampling the kernel's own output at the bars\n// where it is non-`na` gives exactly what Pine sampled, with no dynamic offset.\n//\n// The two `ta.pivothigh` calls below are identical and collapse to ONE node under\n// CSE, so this reads as two calls and costs one.\n//\n// BOUND YOUR INPUTS. `left`/`right` fed from an `input.int` with no `maxval` are\n// priced at `maximumLookback` (20,000 ops/bar) by admission — see gap G9. Every\n// caller should pass an input declared with BOTH `minval=` and `maxval=`.\n\nlast_high(left, right) => ta.valuewhen(not na(ta.pivothigh(high, left, right)), ta.pivothigh(high, left, right), 0)\nlast_low(left, right) => ta.valuewhen(not na(ta.pivotlow(low, left, right)), ta.pivotlow(low, left, right), 0)\n"),
